{"title":"Viviani's theorem","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><b>Viviani's Theorem</b> states that for an equilateral triangle, the sum of the altitudes from an arbitrary point chosen within or on the triangle is equal to the height from a vertex of the triangle to the other side.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Viviani%27s_theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> be an equilateral triangle and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be a point inside the triangle.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/1/0f198ae3c5a5ee95665253d78074fbdcd1d0a8c2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]  /* Geogebra to Asymptote conversion, documentation at artofproblemsolving.com/Wiki, go to User:Azjps/geogebra */ import graph; size(8.cm);  real labelscalefactor = 0.5; /* changes label-to-point distance */ pen dps = linewidth(0.7) + fontsize(10); defaultpen(dps); /* default pen style */  pen dotstyle = black; /* point style */  real xmin = -3.88, xmax = 30.12, ymin = -5.5, ymax = 11.;  /* image dimensions */ pen zzttqq = rgb(0.6,0.2,0.); pen qqwuqq = rgb(0.,0.39215686274509803,0.);   draw((3.22,-0.78)--(14.52,-0.7)--(8.800717967697247,9.046087062764157)--cycle, zzttqq);  draw((10.222698397686688,-0.3061486732450197)--(9.798444960927041,-0.30915223739907016)--(9.801448525081092,-0.7334056741587179)--(10.22570196184074,-0.7304021100046675)--cycle, qqwuqq);  draw((6.871598765286669,4.790362710570685)--(7.08112432080719,5.15927874652442)--(6.712208284853454,5.368804302044939)--(6.502682729332935,4.9998882660912045)--cycle, qqwuqq);  draw((11.476061820875268,3.6488294940564145)--(11.690789702114396,3.28291702225673)--(12.05670217391408,3.4976449034958583)--(11.841974292674951,3.8635573752955428)--cycle, qqwuqq);   /* draw figures */ draw((3.22,-0.78)--(14.52,-0.7), zzttqq);  draw((14.52,-0.7)--(8.800717967697247,9.046087062764157), zzttqq);  draw((8.800717967697247,9.046087062764157)--(3.22,-0.78), zzttqq);  draw((10.2,2.9)--(10.22570196184074,-0.7304021100046675));  draw((10.2,2.9)--(6.502682729332935,4.9998882660912045));  draw((10.2,2.9)--(11.841974292674951,3.8635573752955428));   /* dots and labels */ dot((3.22,-0.78),dotstyle);  label(&quot;$A$&quot;, (3.3,-0.58), NW * labelscalefactor);  dot((14.52,-0.7),dotstyle);  label(&quot;$B$&quot;, (14.6,-0.5), NE * labelscalefactor);  dot((8.800717967697247,9.046087062764157),dotstyle);  label(&quot;$C$&quot;, (8.88,9.24), N * labelscalefactor);  dot((10.2,2.9),dotstyle);  label(&quot;$P$&quot;, (10.28,3.1), NE * labelscalefactor);  dot((6.502682729332935,4.9998882660912045),linewidth(3.pt) + dotstyle);  label(&quot;$B&#39;$&quot;, (6.58,5.12), NW * labelscalefactor);  dot((11.841974292674951,3.8635573752955428),linewidth(3.pt) + dotstyle);  label(&quot;$A&#39;$&quot;, (11.92,3.98), NE * labelscalefactor);  dot((10.22570196184074,-0.7304021100046675),linewidth(3.pt) + dotstyle);  label(&quot;$C&#39;$&quot;, (10.3,-0.62), NE * labelscalefactor);  label(&quot;$x$&quot;, (9.88,1.1), NE * labelscalefactor);  label(&quot;$z$&quot;, (8.5,4.24), NE * labelscalefactor);  label(&quot;$y$&quot;, (11.18,3.12), NE * labelscalefactor);  clip((xmin,ymin)--(xmin,ymax)--(xmax,ymax)--(xmax,ymin)--cycle);   /* end of picture */[/asy]\" width=\"412\" height=\"355\" />\nWe label the altitudes from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to each of sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/0/840e2b592390eb6ec918fa6f3292716ce170de66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/3/e33fe7d65facd8868f58b6e94ddc7f153a5a3f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/5/12517bbc31cb9026c71299b7a0d232d07e60f284.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> respectively. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is equilateral, we can say that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/e/80e5ef17700172e912e5fac1f847bcf33d2ab704.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s=AB=BC=AC$\" width=\"166\" height=\"13\" />. Therefore, using the fact that the area of a triangle is half the product of its base and altitude, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/b/62b69c0699bc641ca53ef8d0b401bc5dc507a980.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[ABP]=\\dfrac{sx}{2},\\ [BCP]=\\dfrac{sy}{2},\\text{ and }[ACP]=\\dfrac{sz}{2}.\\]\" width=\"358\" height=\"32\" /> But we also have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/7/6b7488e59253a02cc677381a389700bea38ed51f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[ABC]=\\dfrac{as}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"97\" height=\"33\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is the height of the equilateral triangle. Observe that the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> can also be expressed as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/6/1b62228b4c197139510fa7b165d73409b8f0a792.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[ABC]=[ABP]+[BCP]+[ACP]=\\dfrac{sx}{2}+\\dfrac{sy}{2}+\\dfrac{sz}{2}=\\dfrac{s}{2}(x+y+z).\\]\" width=\"536\" height=\"32\" /> Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/c/21c964005c27ff176ff1ccff6194b58e68041b70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{s}{2}(x+y+z)=\\dfrac{s}{2}(a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"160\" height=\"33\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/1/821911494f415cfa70db4fb19cd8c55a688f6515.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y+z=a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"107\" height=\"14\" />. This is Viviani's theorem - the sum of the altitudes' lengths from any point within the triangle is constant - and this constant is equal to the length of the altitude from a vertex.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Example_Problems\">Example Problems</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Viviani%27s_theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Example Problems\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>1. Equilateral triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> has incircle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d7d48553f4d9e7ab418118607ea324cbfddfda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> lies on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d7d48553f4d9e7ab418118607ea324cbfddfda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> are on sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/5/e657259c0995d2c36d46744bb4aa81471e6575ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CA$\" width=\"28\" height=\"13\" />, respectively, such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/d/f3db0895645c793ac130325cd9f0e5b9db64e30b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PD\\perp AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"84\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/1/651a5328ae70e8e2418182350414253a5e2c8198.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PE\\perp BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"84\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/1/f616b0ce9ee964f98344f2f6797c5c7774389394.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PF\\perp CA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"82\" height=\"14\" />. Given that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/a/6eaf4ed8e289051ee4c0e276d8418573d927904c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PD=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"65\" height=\"14\" />, compute <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/b/91b8a6e358bfe1002067159013aad94976f0ea86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PE+PF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"81\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p>(ans: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/6/b7616a230c77f0d9a577e5bca01aa06f1c35c457.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$17$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />)\n</p><p>2. Point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> lies inside equilateral triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/812a5bf65a623b4fb2fddedb8c99a2b981265d15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{T}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. The perpendiculars from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to the sides of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/812a5bf65a623b4fb2fddedb8c99a2b981265d15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{T}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> divide <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/812a5bf65a623b4fb2fddedb8c99a2b981265d15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{T}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> into three quadrilaterals with perimeters <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Find the perimeter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/812a5bf65a623b4fb2fddedb8c99a2b981265d15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{T}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>(ans: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/e/bee74bbc01ed68b870160ad611908e1e820fe52a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$18-6\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"21\" />)\n</p><p>3. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> be an equilateral triangle with side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is a point outside the triangle, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/f/05fc2c59d25e97ec515446444b44cbe415d3f383.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PX$\" width=\"29\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/0/a0080013cb20903a6f4e80fa9450edd707894926.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/3/4738a4b20671143d64672cc4a0ec509acd656323.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PZ$\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" /> are the altitudes from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, respectively. A diagram is shown, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is on the opposite side of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. Suppose that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/b/67b6b51f30d735c215854f0def507ca5295655de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PX=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"66\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/a/d9ac2975d0d0e2e38baba3fdd490b42b8e1b7593.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PZ=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"63\" height=\"14\" />. What is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/0/a0080013cb20903a6f4e80fa9450edd707894926.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/8/7c8194ee49519b2953ce34de23c757ea4c127d5b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import geometry; point A,B,C,P,X,Y,Z; A=(0,0); B=(1,sqrt(3)); C=(2,0); P=(4sqrt(3)/3,2); X=(5sqrt(3)/6,5/2); Y=((9-sqrt(3))/6,(1+sqrt(3))/2); Z=(4sqrt(3)/3,0); draw(B--C--A--X--P--Z--C); draw(Y--P); label(&quot;A&quot;,A,SW); label(&quot;B&quot;,B,NW); label(&quot;C&quot;,C,S); label(&quot;P&quot;,P,NE); label(&quot;X&quot;,X,NW); label(&quot;Y&quot;,Y,SW); label(&quot;Z&quot;,Z,S); [/asy]\" width=\"235\" height=\"252\" />\n</p><p>(ans: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/6/c36555905f88b6295c49f76f2f7f7ed4c529b330.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3-\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"57\" height=\"21\" />)\n</p><p>4. Consider the equilateral triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> with sides of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/1/071c5581ae64f649154db85ae9a400ce45b191d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8\\sqrt{3}\\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"62\" height=\"21\" />. A point in the interior of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> is said to be “special” if it is a distance of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/f/a2fd01dce4d24d42ee322b3591970b190897b399.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> from one side of the triangle and a distance of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/f/5ef65994819018f68c862d01bdff5232ae17d51d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7\\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> from another side. Consider the convex polygon whose vertices consist of the special points. What is the area of this polygon?'\n</p><p>(ans: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/c/69c1bcd85ad54d45e285afea99fb8e9150f83273.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"21\" />)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Viviani%27s_theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018034556\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.023 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.032 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 359/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 15287/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:11534:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018034556 and revision id 270371. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}